Source audit changes the narrow question: RER1 loss is a stronger discriminator than a generic total-protein increase. PMC8191293 Results p0175 reports higher surface fraction but lower total levels for WT and mutants (possible N41Q exception). The absolute surface change cannot be inferred from opposite-direction marginal summaries. PMC4227013 Fig 3 instead shows L16P moving to Lamp1-positive late endosomes/lysosomes after Rer1 knockdown, not demonstrated myelin incorporation. G150D fails this redistribution and the double Rer1/calnexin perturbation. The proposed test will explicitly separate productive delivery from denominator loss/degradative escape after RER1 depletion.

UGGT1 correction verified from primary Results p0170 and Discussion p0210: its KO decreases efficiency and total levels for glycosylated PMP22, unlike glycan-deficient N41Q. The paper abstract's statement that UGGT1 limits trafficking contradicts its own detailed results; do not propagate that abstract wording. Current PXD023091 projectDescription independently says UGGT1 promotes trafficking, corroborating the direction rather than resolving function. LMAN1 strong co-IP but little trafficking phenotype is a negative functional follow-up, not proof that LMAN1 can never matter. Calnexin WT/N41Q versus L16P responses are different; HeLa GFP knockdown versus HEK293 extracellular-myc knockout are not equivalent experiments.

Both main papers and supplements contain figures/summary statistics but not a native paired surface/total synthesis-calibrated myelin-rescue matrix. The newly retrieved co-IP workbook is a selected interaction export, not that matrix. Its safe scalar/native-cell audit will preserve accession, literal Gene values, source rows and selection status rather than infer missing equals absent.

